# Styling Bullets with CSS 🎨

The `list-style-type` property controls bullet shapes and markers:

```css_not
ul.square {
  list-style-type: square;
}

ul.circle {
  list-style-type: circle;
}

ul.custom-none {
  list-style-type: none; /* Removes bullets completely */
  padding-left: 0;
}

ol.roman {
  list-style-type: upper-roman;
}
```

# Custom Icon & Emoji Bullets ⭐

Using the modern CSS `::marker` pseudo-element, you can style or replace bullets with custom emojis:

```css_not
ul.emoji-list li::marker {
  content: "🚀 ";
  font-size: 1.2rem;
}

ul.check-list li::marker {
  content: "✅ ";
}
```

```html_not
<ul class="emoji-list">
  <li>Fast performance</li>
  <li>Global distribution</li>
</ul>
```

# Building Navigation Bars from Lists 🧭

Semantically, a navigation bar is an unordered list of links!

```html_not
<nav class="navbar">
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/">Home</a></li>
    <li><a href="/courses">Courses</a></li>
    <li><a href="/about">About</a></li>
    <li><a href="/contact">Contact</a></li>
  </ul>
</nav>
```

```css_not
.navbar ul {
  list-style: none;
  display: flex;       /* Horizontal layout */
  gap: 24px;
  padding: 0;
  margin: 0;
}

.navbar a {
  text-decoration: none;
  color: #3b82f6;
  font-weight: 500;
}
```

# List Spacing & Box Model 📐

By default, web browsers apply built-in styling to `<ul>` and `<ol>`:
- `margin-top: 1em;`
- `margin-bottom: 1em;`
- `padding-left: 40px;` (provides room for the bullets/numbers!)

```css_not
/* Common list reset for custom UIs */
.clean-list {
  list-style: none;
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
}
```

> ⚠️ **Gotcha:** If you remove `list-style: none` without resetting `padding-left`, your text will still be pushed 40px to the right!

# The `list-style` Shorthand 🖌️

The `list-style` shorthand sets `type`, `position`, and `image` in one declaration:

```css_not
/* Syntax: list-style: [type] [position] [image] */
ul {
  list-style: square inside;
}
```

## `list-style-position`:
- `outside` *(Default)*: Bullet sits outside the content flow box.
- `inside`: Bullet sits inside the content box and wraps along with the text.
